Mon Sep  1 09:50:00 UTC 2026 - Patrick Haar <Pihaar@users.noreply.github.com>

- Secrets/config moved out of code, git and the package into an untracked
  env file at /etc/busradar/busradar.env (systemd EnvironmentFile, optional).
  It carries the CARTO Basemaps API key (BUSRADAR_CARTO_KEY) and the HAFAS
  connection data (BUSRADAR_HAFAS_SOURCES, a JSON array of one or more
  sources). New module busradar_config.py loads them lazily and fail-closed:
  a missing/invalid config degrades (no vehicles, map via OSM fallback)
  instead of crashing; source endpoints are validated as https (SSRF/SEC-375).
  The former hardcoded HAFAS endpoint/AID/client in hafas.py and
  stops_builder.py are gone; a placeholder template ships as
  /opt/busradar/busradar.env.example. CARTO now requires an API key for
  raster basemaps — the key is injected server-side into a <meta> tag and
  appended to the tile URLs at runtime (no inline script → CSP hash
  unaffected). /api/health gains hafas_configured + hafas_sources.

-------------------------------------------------------------------
Mon Aug  3 11:26:25 UTC 2026 - Patrick Haar <Pihaar@users.noreply.github.com>

- Social sharing: add Open Graph + Twitter Card meta tags and a 1200x630
  preview image (static/og-image.png, a screenshot of the live map around
  Sandhausen), so a shared link renders a preview card in WhatsApp,
  Facebook, X and the like instead of a bare URL. The preview language is
  German (canonical, matching the title/description and the BW audience)
  with an og:locale:alternate en_US hint; social crawlers read the raw
  HTML without JS and do not negotiate language, so a single canonical
  preview locale is the robust choice. The image ships as a normal static
  asset (served at /og-image.png); no inline script changed, so the CSP
  hash is unaffected.

-------------------------------------------------------------------
Mon Aug  3 10:36:30 UTC 2026 - Patrick Haar <Pihaar@users.noreply.github.com>

- Stationboard: at a multi-Steig "Drehscheibe" stop, show a hint when
  none of the shown departures could be resolved to the opened Steig.
  Every row then falls open to both Steige, so the list is not actually
  Steig-filtered; the hint makes that transparent instead of presenting
  a silently unfiltered list as if it were filtered. Purely informative
  (no departures are hidden), and it disappears as soon as at least one
  row is resolved to the opened Steig.

-------------------------------------------------------------------
Mon Aug  3 09:23:27 UTC 2026 - Patrick Haar <Pihaar@users.noreply.github.com>

- Search: allow searching by stop ID. A pure-digit query is treated as
  an extId lookup (exact matches first, then prefix), so pasting a stop
  ID jumps straight to that stop. Exact IDs surface even when a same-named
  sibling shares the coordinate (the ID path skips the name/coordinate
  dedupe). Falls back to the normal name search when the digits are not an
  extId; the name-search path is otherwise unchanged.

-------------------------------------------------------------------
Mon Aug  3 08:53:54 UTC 2026 - Patrick Haar <Pihaar@users.noreply.github.com>

- Stationboard: make the Steig filter engage immediately on open instead
  of after ~30 s. Two causes are fixed: the initial board is fetched over
  the REST endpoint, which was not tagged (only the SSE push was), so the
  first render always fell open to both Steige; and the tags were only
  resolved lazily on the first tick after open. The REST /api/stationboard
  now runs the same platform-tagging as the SSE push (shared helper), and
  a small background warmer keeps the Steig cache warm for the configured
  families (at startup and every 10 min, gated on the stops-rebuild flag),
  so an opened multi-Steig board is already platform-filtered from a warm
  cache. Unresolved rows still fail open (shown on both Steige).

-------------------------------------------------------------------
Mon Aug  3 08:26:35 UTC 2026 - Patrick Haar <Pihaar@users.noreply.github.com>

- Stationboard: fix the Steig tags never reaching the client. The
  server attached the per-row platform array at the SSE payload level
  while the client reads it from the board object (data.stationboard),
  so the board always fell open to showing both Steige. The tags now
  ride inside the board object (shallow copy, the shared board cache is
  not mutated), so the opened Steig marker is filtered to its own
  departures as intended. Packaging fix: the new steig.py module is now
  installed by the RPM (%files + install list) — it was missing, which
  crashed the service on first boot of the previous build.

-------------------------------------------------------------------
Sun Aug  2 21:36:01 UTC 2026 - Patrick Haar <Pihaar@users.noreply.github.com>

- Stationboard: show platform-accurate ("Steig") departures at
  multi-platform "Drehscheibe" stops such as Walldorf-Drehscheibe,
  where the HAFAS station board is byte-identical for both Steige and
  cannot be separated by board data alone. The Steig of each individual
  departure is resolved per departure from its JourneyDetails (matched
  by scheduled time), so a line that passes the stop twice in one trip
  or runs differently at night is placed correctly — no generalisation
  from one course to the whole line. The server tags each board row and
  the client filters the opened Steig marker to its own departures;
  unresolved rows fail open (shown on both Steige, never hidden). Which
  stops are treated this way is a central config (steig.py) — a new
  multi-Steig stop is one entry, verified against HAFAS. Resolution is
  a bounded background JourneyDetails backfill (own concurrency limit,
  singleflight, ephemeral 6h cache) that never blocks the board push;
  the next ~10s tick carries the resolved tags. /api/health gains a
  per-family resolution rate as an early warning if the upstream Steig
  field ever breaks.
- Security hardening carried in the same change: the map stop tooltip
  is now a text node instead of a string (closes a stored-XSS path
  through untrusted HAFAS stop names in Leaflet's innerHTML tooltip),
  and location-ID validation rejects a smuggled second "@L=" field
  (case-insensitive) that could otherwise query a foreign station under
  the opened stop's name.
- Internal: the triplicate A×-GID platform parser (stops builder ×2,
  vehicle flattening) is consolidated into one hafas.extract_platform
  helper, so a HAFAS GID-format change is a single fix instead of three.


- Stationboard: fix the departure/arrival board so a selected platform
  shows its own departures from the initial view. At multi-platform
  stops (e.g. Wiesloch-Walldorf Bahnhof) the paging horizon, slice,
  line filter and load-more now operate on the platform-scoped board
  instead of the full station board, so the initial view is no longer
  empty when the next station-wide departure belongs to another
  platform, and the line filter matches what is shown. The scope
  decision is derived once (locL geometry, stable across reconciles
  and refetches) and shared by every consumer.
- Load-more now stays available at busy stops whose board hit the
  HAFAS 50-entry cap, as long as the selected platform has cached
  departures beyond the current horizon (the 50-cap hint no longer
  suppresses client-side pagination).

